WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:07.920
Thanks to CuriosityStream for supporting this&nbsp;
episode! Go to CuriosityStream.com/microcosmos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:12.534
to start streaming thousands of&nbsp;
documentaries and non fiction TV shows.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.400
We’ve spent most of our journey through the&nbsp;
microcosmos seeking out the organisms that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.840
are too small to see with the human eye.&nbsp;
The bacteria, the ciliates, the tardigrades.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.360
And Part of what makes them so&nbsp;
exciting to find is that they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.480
so tiny. Every moment we spend with one of&nbsp;
these organisms is a peek into something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:58.160
exceptional in our experience of the world,&nbsp;
and it’s the result of how much work James,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:03.840
our master of microscopes, has to put into&nbsp;
hunting down as many microbes as he can.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.320
And sometimes, that effort&nbsp;
requires a lot of persistence.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:13.006
Take the creature we’re going to&nbsp;
focus on today: the bristle worm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.880
This has been one of the white whales for our&nbsp;
channel for some time. And as you watch it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.400
you can perhaps understand&nbsp;
why we have been searching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:30.400
so hard for it. It’s got the body of a pipe&nbsp;
cleaner with the head of a cartoon dragon.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.000
And maybe you also understand&nbsp;
that just because we’ve been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:40.080
wanting to find one of these worms, that doesn’t&nbsp;
mean we are guaranteed anything. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.408
one of the things you have to&nbsp;
accept about the microcosmos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.408 --> 00:01:44.480
and microbe hunting,

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:51.280
is that it is a big world full of tiny creatures,&nbsp;
and it can take a while to find some of them.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.080
The fact that we are showing you&nbsp;
one of those bristle worms right now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:00.315
spoils the twist we would usually build&nbsp;
into, but, surprise, we found a bristle worm!

00:02:00.315 --> 00:02:04.960
The real twist is that this was&nbsp;
not the first bristleworm we found.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:10.720
This was the first bristle worm we found.&nbsp;
Twenty centimeters of segments and bristles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.960
climbing up the side of a tank before&nbsp;
burrowing back beneath the sand.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:19.920
Unfortunately, that’s just about all the video&nbsp;
we got of that bristle worm.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.440
James spent so much time trying to&nbsp;
find this bristle worm again that he

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:28.634
started to feel a connection to it. So&nbsp;
he decided to give it a name: Gunther.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:34.080
James spent hours trying to catch it&nbsp;
without hurting the worm. But Gunther&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:37.520
has hundreds of appendages&nbsp;
that can grab onto sand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:41.840
and James didn’t want to accidentally&nbsp;
snap the worm in half with his tweezers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:47.120
So instead of showing all the features of Gunther&nbsp;
that we wanted to show, we’re going to show clips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:53.360
of this bristle worm instead because it kind&nbsp;
of resembles Gunther, except a lot tinier.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Gunther is hopefully still somewhere in that&nbsp;
tank, living a nice life in the burrow it dug&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.040
for itself. But it’s hard to know exactly where&nbsp;
in the tank Gunther made its home because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.080
would only show activity in the dark. The moment&nbsp;
James tried to turn the lights on and watch the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:15.840
worm in motion, it would vanish again under the&nbsp;
sand. And as it became harder and harder to find,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.680
James kept hoping that one day, something&nbsp;
would bring another bristle worm to him.

00:03:21.332 --> 00:03:26.400
And then, one day, James received a&nbsp;
package from a coral farm containing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.560
sediments and other things that might&nbsp;
contain some interesting organisms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.320
We talked about some of those organisms&nbsp;
that James found in our last episode.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:38.400
But in addition to all of those&nbsp;
organisms, James found several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.189
species of his long sought after bristle worm.

00:03:46.165 --> 00:03:48.640
Bristle worms are also known as polychaetes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:52.880
and they’re part of the segmented&nbsp;
worm phylum known as Annelids.

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:58.880
The name “polychaete” translates in Greek to&nbsp;
“many hairs.” Those stiff hairs are called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:02.160
setae, and for most polychaetes,&nbsp;
they’re attached to paddle-like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:06.647
appendages called the parapodia that&nbsp;
branch off each segment of the worm.

00:04:07.287 --> 00:04:11.840
Our giant friend Gunther most likely&nbsp;
belongs to the order Eunicida,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:17.840
but its microscopic look-alike is more unknown&nbsp;
to us. But we can imagine that it spends its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:23.920
life crawling around the sand and feeding on&nbsp;
algae, or whatever else it can take a bite out of.&nbsp;

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:28.720
You can see this one moving its mouth in&nbsp;
slow motion, like a weird pair of pincers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:34.212
inside clamping down on something, and the&nbsp;
movement is even more dramatic in full speed.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.240
This probably looks like a bunch of&nbsp;
tiny individual orange worms tangled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:45.520
together. But it is, in fact, a worm belonging&nbsp;
to the genus Cirratulus that can get to around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:50.880
12 centimeters in length. And along with&nbsp;
its distinctive color, the worm is easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:56.440
to spot in wet sandy mud because of those&nbsp;
threads you see waving across your screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:01.600
Some of these threads are tentacles,&nbsp;
but others are actually gills.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:08.240
And in the water, those threads seem to float&nbsp;
serenely. But this effect is lost on land.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:13.440
In a paper from the beginning of the 20th&nbsp;
century titled “Notes on the Ecology of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:18.480
Cirratulus tentaculatus,” the author&nbsp;
wrote, “When withdrawn from the mud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.840
Cirratulus presents an exceeding&nbsp;
limp and bedraggled appearance.”

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:31.280
This worm has a more imposing appearance&nbsp;
but also a funnier name. It belongs to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:36.560
terebellid order, but it’s known as a spaghetti&nbsp;
worm. These tropical worms live in sand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:42.640
building tubes out of gravel and limestone to&nbsp;
live in. Their tentacles spill out from the tube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:48.623
sometimes extending as far as a meter to gather&nbsp;
building materials and food for the worm.

00:05:49.194 --> 00:05:54.960
To reproduce, spaghetti worms will release their&nbsp;
eggs and sperm into the water, but only at night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.960 --> 00:06:00.960
and sometimes even without other males or females&nbsp;
around. They seem to do this on a lunar cycle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:06.240
with a limited two week window for these&nbsp;
gametes to release and find each other. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:11.840
seems like an extraordinarily chance-y way to&nbsp;
reproduce, but it seems to work for this worm.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:16.800
These are the bristle worms we were able&nbsp;
to find from our coral farm samples. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:21.040
so it seems like our hunt for&nbsp;
this white whale might be over.

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:26.640
Except, the more we’ve been reading about&nbsp;
bristle worms, the more we wish we could find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:34.160
even more. Because these are an animal whose&nbsp;
existence seems designed to inspire lists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:39.920
of fun facts and bizarre trivia. There&nbsp;
are thousands of species of polychaetes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:44.000
and they all seem to have something&nbsp;
remarkable or weird about them.&nbsp;

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:50.960
Some spend their lives in tubes that stick up&nbsp;
from the sand, using their parapodia to paddle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:56.640
water through the burrow. Others have managed&nbsp;
to carve out lives near hydrothermal vents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:02.640
Some sound like they’re straight out of a horror&nbsp;
novel, growing up to ten feet long or dining on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:08.480
the bones of decomposing animals. And some&nbsp;
even have eyes on both ends of their bodies.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:13.040
So knowing that all of these&nbsp;
different species are out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:18.520
how could we ever end our quest&nbsp;
for the bristle worm?

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:29.840
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

